# Production config for the WORKSTATION-embedded POS bundle (`vite build --mode
# workstation`, served by the workstation at /pos/). Committed on purpose: it is
# safe to run in production and is the single source of truth for what the
# embedded bundle bakes in — a developer's gitignored .env.local (localhost)
# must NEVER leak into this build. Vite loads `.env.workstation` at HIGHER
# priority than `.env.local`, so this wins.
#
# KEEP THIS FILE COMMITTED. It spent a day untracked, and during that day the
# same `make build` produced two different bundles: with the file present the
# embedded POS baked https://tempo.godx.jp, without it (a fresh clone, CI) it
# fell through to .env.local / the code default. "Works on my machine" then means
# nothing — the build is only reproducible if this file travels with the repo.

# Cloud-mode backend — a FALLBACK, not the primary target.
#
# Two things now stand in front of it:
#   1. The embedded build defaults to WORKSTATION mode (base-url-resolver.ts
#      defaultMode): the page was served by the workstation, so the LAN gateway
#      is same-origin and reachable by construction. Cloud mode is an explicit
#      operator choice in Settings → Connection.
#   2. Even in Cloud mode, a current workstation injects
#      `<meta name="x-pos-cloud-url">` from its own WS_APP_CLOUD_URL and that
#      wins over this value (#1511) — one .env on the shop PC drives both
#      pairing (via the relay) and Cloud mode, with no pos-web rebuild.
#
# So this is what an OLD workstation binary (no meta injection) falls back to in
# Cloud mode. It must be the production host, never localhost:5400.
#
# History worth keeping: when the default mode was "cloud" and no meta was read,
# this single line decided where EVERY /api/v1/pos/* call went. A terminal paired
# through a workstation syncing with another backend sent its calls here, got
# rejected, and the payment dialog reported "no checkout payment methods
# configured" — a connection fault wearing a configuration fault's clothes.
VITE_API_URL=https://tempo.godx.jp

# Leave UNSET. This pins the resolver (isModeForced) and makes the Settings →
# Connection toggle inert, which also kills the shift-gate's "switch side"
# escape hatch when one side is down. The workstation build already defaults to
# LAN mode in code, so pinning buys nothing and costs the escape hatch.
VITE_POS_API_MODE=

# No baked single-shop default. An UNPAIRED terminal must land on /pairing, not a
# dev shop slug. The paired device's own branch_slug supplies the real shop at
# runtime (App.tsx DefaultShopRedirect), so leave this empty in production.
VITE_SHOP_SLUG=

# Deliberately unset: for this build the workstation is `location.origin` (the
# host that served the page), which beats any build-time or stored address —
# see getWorkstationUrl(). A value here would be ignored by the /pos build.
VITE_WORKSTATION_API_URL=
